# GLM tool calls stream while they decode. With 0053 the chat stream held each call and sent it whole, header and
# arguments in one delta, at its </tool_call>, with an empty delta every 2 s before that. The Messages and Responses
# streams drop empty deltas, and the empty deltas reset 0111's keep-alive timer, so a long call sent no byte at all
# there: on two Sparks a Write call with 28,490 characters of content was silent for 128.5 s on /v1/messages, then came
# as one input_json_delta. An agent client's live token count stops for the whole call.
# cuda/reply_text.py, GlmCallStreamer: a call's header (index, id, name, empty arguments) goes out when its name is
# whole and names an offered tool. A string value goes out as JSON-escaped pieces while it decodes. A tail that can
# begin </arg_value> or </tool_call> waits, and the stream decoder already holds a partial UTF-8 character. A typed or
# nullable value goes out at its </arg_value>, as decode_parameter reads it. The closing brace waits for the block's
# </tool_call>. Then parse_tool_calls reads the block, and the call continues from what went out to the parser's
# arguments, so the pieces join to the whole reply's arguments byte for byte (key order, escapes, types, unknown
# keys). A call the parser can read otherwise (an <arg_key> the model left out, text after </arg_key>) holds its
# pieces, and the parser's arguments go out at the closer. A repeated key goes out twice: the call closes when its
# JSON decodes to the parser's object. A call whose sent pieces the parser does not continue gets no brace; it stays
# open and the server logs a line. unmask (0117) runs on a call's whole arguments, so a spelled tag that two pieces
# split comes back whole.
# cuda/server.py: a round is cut after each </tool_call> token, and an open call's pieces to its closer go out before
# the round's text, so no text delta comes between a call's header and its last piece (that ends the call for
# LiteLLM's Messages stream). A round's text still goes out in one delta when no call delta comes between. At the
# finish the calls go out as the stream sent them, then the calls of blocks the streamer skipped (JSON, Qwen) under
# the next indexes; the handler sends no call twice. The finish reason is the parser's.
# A reply that ends inside a call (max_tokens, a stop string) ends as the whole reply does: finish_reason length or
# stop, no tool_calls. This drops 0053's rule that a cut call is never sent: its header and pieces went out, and it
# stays open without a closing brace, as vLLM and the Anthropic API stream a cut call. No switch.
